{"id":"dc541abb-4d6f-4bd6-b8e2-7f034a5ffd03","article_key":"doi:10.1016/j.cell.2026.09.052","title":"Contrasting demographic impacts of Roman colonization and early medieval migrations in the Iberian Peninsula","title_zh":"罗马殖民与早期中世纪迁徙对伊比利亚半岛人口结构的对比影响","doi":"10.1016/j.cell.2026.09.052","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cell.2026.09.052","abstract":"It has been unclear how Roman and later Germanic political control shaped the demography of Iberia and how it differed from other parts of Europe. We report genetic data from 243 ancient individuals dating to 100-800 CE, analyzed alongside 58 previously published individuals. In Roman times, we document an influx of people with ancestry from the Eastern Mediterranean and an influx of North Africans, especially in the center and south. Beginning in the 5th century, Germanic and Alan (Sarmatian) groups assumed political control. Although we identify individuals with Central-Northern European-associated ancestry, including siblings buried ∼725 km apart, the Hispano-Roman population remained continuous in Iberia. These demographic patterns contrast sharply with those in Britain, where major demographic change followed Rome's collapse, and with those in the central Mediterranean, where both periods were associated with profound transformations. Together, these findings highlight regional variation in admixture and continuity across post-Roman Europe.","abstract_zh":"关于罗马及后期日耳曼政治统治如何塑造伊比利亚的人口结构，以及其与欧洲其他地区的差异，尚不清楚。我们报告了来自243名古代个体的遗传数据，这些个体的年代为公元100-800年，并将其与58名先前发表的个体一起进行分析。在罗马时期，我们记录了来自东地中海地区的血统人群涌入，以及北非人群的涌入，尤其在中部和南部地区。自5世纪起，日耳曼和阿兰（萨尔马提亚）群体开始掌握政治控制。尽管我们识别出具有中北欧相关血统的个体，包括相距约725公里的兄妹，但伊比利亚的伊比利亚-罗马人群体仍保持连续性。这些人口模式与英国形成鲜明对比，后者在罗马崩溃后出现了重大人口变动；以及与中部地中海地区形成对比，后者在两个时期都伴随深刻的转变。综上所述，这些发现突显了后罗马欧洲各地区在混合与连续性方面的区域差异。","snippet":"It has been unclear how Roman and later Germanic political control shaped the demography of Iberia and how it differed from other parts of Europe. We report genetic data from 243 ancient individuals dating to 100-800 CE, analyzed alongside 58 previously published individuals. In Roman times, we document an influx of people with ancestry from the Eastern Mediterranean and an influx of North Africans, especially in th…","translated_at":"2026-10-25T02:18:33.046128Z","translation_model":"openai/gpt-oss-20b","source_category":"doi","article_type":"journal-article","volume":null,"issue":null,"published_at":"2026-10-01T00:00:00","acquired_at":"2026-10-24T18:36:01Z","display_date":"2026-10-24T18:36:01Z","display_date_source":"acquired","online_published_at":null,"print_published_at":"2026-10-01T00:00:00","first_author":"Pablo Carrión","authors_text":"Pablo Carrión, Iñigo Olalde, Michael McCormick, Kyle Harper, Maite I. Garcia-Collado, Vanessa Villalba-Mouco, Juan Manuel Jiménez-Arenas, Neus Coromina, David Vivó, Josep María Vergès, Ana Costa, Daniel Botella Ortega, Macarena Bustamante-Álvarez, Javier Heras-Mora, Ricardo Ortega-Ruiz, Celia Chaves, Juan Antonio Quirós-Castillo, Jordi Roig, José Suárez-Padilla, Ildelfonso Navarro-Luengo, Miguel Ángel Cuadrado-Prieto, Isidro Aguilera, Jordi Morera, Raúl Catalán, María Luisa Cerdeño, Josep F. Roig-Pérez, Moisés Díaz-García, Paula Chirosa-Cañavate, Tatiana Piza-Ruiz, Elena Vallejo-Casas, Sergio Vidal-Álvarez, Josep Burch, Jordi Sagrera, Jordi Vivo, Adrià Cubo-Córdoba, Virgilio Martínez-Enamorado, Francisca Rengel-Castro, Virginia García-Entero, Alicia Rodero, Enrique Viguera, Nadin Rohland, Juan Ignacio Morales, María Soto, Swapan Mallick, Artur Cebrià, Pablo García-Borja, Paz Calduch Bardoll, Pilar Ulloa-Eres, Andrés Carretero-Pérez, Teresa Gómez-Espinosa, Beatriz Campderá Gutiérrez, Paula Pagés-Alonso, Consuelo Vara-Izquierdo, José Martínez-Peñarroya, Samuel Sardà-Seuma, José Manuel Castaño-Aguilar, Sonia López-Chamizo, Ron Pinhasi, Carles Lalueza-Fox, David Reich","authors":[{"position":0,"given_name":"Pablo","family_name":"Carrión","full_name":"Pablo Carrión","affiliation":null},{"position":1,"given_name":"Iñigo","family_name":"Olalde",